7797f4fc073","q-db3339437f5c","q-b8b2387e6681"],"size":40},{"id":"exam-9bf9de33873d","title":"Exam Set 1201 · Human Anatomy","subject":"Human Anatomy","questionIds":["q-9fae70a325b4","q-8951defa8548","q-458759927382","q-d1d638c381a9","q-a442181dec26","q-634e43815366","q-8c6a650788a5","q-817c6235a6b0","q-9cac93c1958d","q-03aa04a4debb","q-724a656e92e7","q-348aa1cbff50","q-88bc61268e0d","q-2a70ee3f2a06","q-bb85f6502976","q-d0067c3caab1","q-3502663bdfac","q-2801435e7d61","q-79b2beee0f66","q-15d1e57c5f50","q-7fe4cec42133","q-081f6b8f6fdd","q-b588a4a72d8c","q-c5814b326741","q-aa3869f53dc3","q-111a70c814f7","q-dc5a43062f5c","q-415652665c4e","q-12e4efaa92fe","q-2172e1e5ff60","q-1bd00cad172c","q-4b7bd6eeb70f","q-d3f0b92f09a1","q-983475f37769","q-c30718b25a9f","q-85bbb625535b","q-c6b0370b2075","q-47e96347191e","q-0f880bd41e30","q-9d706b6ad3fc","q-314c9e2d918d","q-4f17cd6e76a0","q-8f16081e9da4","q-94516e0e2930","q-bab1ebb6f541","q-90b31afa2458","q-0befba7fa0fa"],"size":47}],"stats":{"originalRecords":30068,"examCollections":1201,"adjudicated":2325,"pending":27743,"quality_unverified_draft":21119,"quality_review_required":5749,"quality_checked_explained":67,"quality_quarantine":1604,"quality_provisional_answer":1529,"batch5CohortScreened":10000,"batch5NewAnswers":213,"previousPreparedAnswers":1057,"latestNewDirectKeys":302,"latestProjectedExactDuplicateKeys":24,"requestedNewAnswerTarget":20252,"newAnswerCountThisTurn":326,"latestCohortNewAnswers":292,"latestCohortUnanswered":9495,"newDirectKeys":168,"newExactDuplicateProjections":9},"answers":{"q-9dc242a05d08":{"correct":"B","answerText":"Mitral valve","explanation":"The mitral (left atrioventricular) valve is bicuspid, with anterior and posterior cusps. Normal tricuspid, pulmonary and aortic valves each have three cusps.","optionReasons":{"A":"Aortic valve typically has three semilunar cusps.","B":"Mitral is the bicuspid valve (anterior and posterior cusps).","C":"Pulmonary valve has three semilunar cusps.","D":"Both pulmonary and aortic valves typically have three cusps.","E":"Tricuspid valve has three leaflets."},"references":[{"title":"OpenStax Anatomy & Physiology 2e — Heart Anatomy","url":"https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/19-1-heart-anatomy"}],"review":"independently_researched_again_2026_10_19","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"8ce5f627726b6811347c7b481f6be2e220fb21f0c5de36fc91df623b86839735"},"q-a0884a917439":{"correct":"B","answerText":"T8","explanation":"The caval opening of the diaphragm is located in its central tendon at vertebral level T8. Esophageal hiatus is T10 and aortic hiatus T12.","optionReasons":{"A":"T12 is the aortic hiatus.","B":"T8 is the vena caval opening.","C":"T10 is the esophageal hiatus.","D":"L1 is below the major diaphragmatic openings.","E":"C7 is a cervical vertebra, not a diaphragmatic level."},"references":[{"title":"NCBI Bookshelf — Anatomy, Thorax: Diaphragm","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519558/"}],"review":"independently_researched_again_2026_10_19","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"ddfd3d15239d84506e215f860eab32404daa50403a20f9c50fa71a7addea2aa5"},"q-3863db90f2ad":{"correct":"C","answerText":"T10","explanation":"The esophageal hiatus traverses the right crus of the diaphragm at T10 and carries the esophagus and vagal trunks.","optionReasons":{"A":"T6 is above the hiatus.","B":"T8 is the caval opening.","C":"T10 is the esophageal hiatus.","D":"T11 is not the standard esophageal opening level.","E":"T12 is the aortic hiatus."},"references":[{"title":"NCBI Bookshelf — Anatomy, Thorax: Diaphragm","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519558/"}],"review":"independently_researched_again_2026_10_19","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"70b1ae62220086b0e037843edfe5ecedf39fdba2f57b9e9184dd08f2e2eef7d5"},"q-cff2bac26598":{"correct":"C","answerText":"T10","explanation":"The esophageal hiatus lies at vertebral level T10. The caval opening is T8 and the aortic hiatus is T12.","optionReasons":{"A":"T6 is superior to the esophageal hiatus.","B":"T8 marks the caval opening.","C":"T10 marks the esophageal hiatus.","D":"T12 marks the aortic hiatus.","E":"L1 is not the esophageal hiatus level."},"references":[{"title":"NCBI Bookshelf — Anatomy, Thorax: Diaphragm","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519558/"}],"review":"independently_researched_again_2026_10_19","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"34cbedd5112ed6e79b0a3980647334438200d2cf46805c969afd8d63105d2c55"},"q-403b50237540":{"correct":"D","answerText":"Right phrenic nerve","explanation":"The esophageal hiatus transmits the esophagus, vagal trunks, esophageal branches of left gastric vessels and lymphatics. Branches of the right phrenic accompany the IVC through the caval opening, not the esophageal hiatus.","optionReasons":{"A":"Vagal trunks do pass through the esophageal hiatus.","B":"Esophageal branches of the left gastric arterial supply accompany it.","C":"Lymphatics can accompany the esophagus through the hiatus.","D":"Right phrenic nerve branches ordinarily accompany the IVC through the caval opening.","E":"Esophageal venous branches of left gastric veins accompany the esophagus."},"references":[{"title":"NCBI Bookshelf — Anatomy, Thorax: Diaphragm","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519558/"}],"review":"independently_researched_again_2026_10_19","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"42b5d866e36359bb60e44d0c9ff405273473537a1e88fc105f28bdc991751633"},"q-5cf391b95c37":{"correct":"E","answerText":"Aortic hiatus opposite T12","explanation":"The aorta traverses the posterior diaphragm at T12; the esophagus passes at T10 and IVC at T8. The phrenic nerve receives roots from C3 to C5.","optionReasons":{"A":"Esophageal opening is T10, not T8.","B":"Phrenic motor roots are predominantly C3–C5, not C5–C7.","C":"Diaphragm contracts for inspiration; quiet expiration is passive.","D":"The caval opening is T8, not T10.","E":"The aortic opening is at T12."},"references":[{"title":"NCBI Bookshelf — Anatomy, Thorax: Diaphragm","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519558/"}],"review":"independently_researched_again_2026_10_19","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"95d4db9f15e1fcbcaa0f96cdbb34f80959c79797c1aedea91f60d31256deb4de"},"q-023d52e1e770":{"correct":"B","answerText":"Vagus nerve","explanation":"The esophageal opening carries the esophagus and vagal trunks; the IVC opening transmits right phrenic branches and the aortic opening carries the aorta, thoracic duct and often azygos vein.","optionReasons":{"A":"Azygos is associated with the aortic opening or right crus, not the usual esophageal opening.","B":"Vagal trunks accompany the esophagus at T10.","C":"Right phrenic branches pass near the caval opening.","D":"Sympathetic trunks pass behind the medial arcuate ligaments.","E":"Thoracic duct passes through the aortic hiatus."},"references":[{"title":"NCBI Bookshelf — Anatomy, Thorax: Diaphragm","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519558/"}],"review":"independently_researched_again_2026_10_19","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"03dfcd9486db7777565c8b16c0f37a670f304e9e3d188b8cf467e437d0fdc7e4"},"q-40f442fd6bc1":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Passes in front of the root of the lung","explanation":"The phrenic nerve descends anterior to the lung root and along the pericardium toward the diaphragm; the vagus descends behind the root.","optionReasons":{"A":"No general rule requires the nerve to approach the midline at every level.","B":"The phrenic nerve also carries sensory fibers from pericardium, mediastinal pleura and diaphragm.","C":"Phrenic nerve passes anterior to the lung root.","D":"T12 is the aortic hiatus; the phrenic traverses the diaphragm independently.","E":"This is not the characteristic division used to identify the phrenic nerve."},"references":[{"title":"NCBI Bookshelf — Anatomy, Thorax: Phrenic Nerves","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK513325/"}],"review":"independently_researched_again_2026_10_19","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"40e6d7d3600f78f928c360998ac69c2cad8a987b4cb4c81fe05256582228e0ab"},"q-d5126048013d":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Diaphragm","explanation":"Contraction of the diaphragm enlarges the thoracic cavity for inspiration. Forced expiration uses abdominal muscles, including the obliques, rectus abdominis and transversus abdominis.","optionReasons":{"A":"Transversus abdominis compresses abdominal contents during forced expiration.","B":"Rectus abdominis contributes to forced expiration by increasing abdominal pressure.","C":"Contraction of the diaphragm drives inspiration, not forced expiration.","D":"External oblique is an abdominal expiratory muscle.","E":"Internal oblique is an abdominal expiratory muscle."},"references":[{"title":"OpenStax — The Process of Breathing","url":"https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology/pages/22-3-the-process-of-breathing"}],"review":"independently_researched_again_2026_10_19","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"865d174a2b8dcee12c8aabe74d376aba77bea9cc6a65dfc46cbad6e5cafe320b"},"q-bf3a30ef9dda":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Phrenic nerves","explanation":"The posterior mediastinum contains the esophagus, thoracic aorta, thoracic duct, azygos system and lymph nodes; phrenic nerves descend anteriorly with the pericardium in the middle mediastinum.","optionReasons":{"A":"Descending thoracic aorta lies in posterior mediastinum.","B":"Thoracic duct courses through posterior mediastinum.","C":"Phrenic nerves descend along pericardium, not within posterior mediastinum.","D":"Azygos venous system is a posterior mediastinal structure.","E":"Posterior mediastinum contains lymph nodes."},"references":[{"title":"NCBI Bookshelf — Anatomy, Thorax: Mediastinum","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK539819/"}],"review":"independently_researched_again_2026_10_19","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"7dfab918c2dfab4d3f13d6d8498cc3ab5b6dc3471437ba4ba4b6e82e2329aa59"},"q-9a03dd8e9e96":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Roots, 7 is incorrect","explanation":"A typical brachial plexus has five roots (C5–T1), three trunks, six divisions, three cords and five major terminal branches.","optionReasons":{"A":"There are five, not seven, brachial plexus roots.","B":"There are three trunks: superior, middle and inferior.","C":"Each of three trunks divides into anterior and posterior divisions: six total.","D":"There are three cords and named peripheral branches."},"references":[{"title":"NCBI Bookshelf — Anatomy, Head and Neck: Brachial Plexus","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK531473/"}],"review":"independently_researched_again_2026_10_19","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"a98a41ee466e8bfc9ed9115ec5f653e3edab782450e84504df0def4be2ca9575"},"q-a66fd74d2c80":{"correct":"A","answerText":"C5 and C6","explanation":"Classic Erb–Duchenne palsy affects the upper trunk of the brachial plexus, predominantly formed by C5 and C6. C7 may also be involved in extended injury.","optionReasons":{"A":"C5–C6 is the classical Erb palsy pattern.","B":"C6–C7 omits C5 and is not the classic upper-trunk lesion.","C":"C2–C3 does not form the usual brachial plexus.","D":"C4–C5 omits the main C6 contribution."},"references":[{"title":"NCBI Bookshelf — Erb Palsy (updated June 22, 2026)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK513260/"}],"review":"independently_researched_again_2026_10_19","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"4a84ced9603294151564392dc80cc3e6315cbf229976943451d28543cac85f8d"},"q-cce5b4bcabc3":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Receives the left jugular and subclavian lymphatic trunks","explanation":"The thoracic duct drains most of the body, receives the left-sided lymphatic trunks and ordinarily terminates at or near the left jugulosubclavian venous junction.","optionReasons":{"A":"The duct normally originates near cisterna chyli around L1–L2, not the T10 vertebral body.","B":"The venous junction is between the internal jugular and subclavian veins, not the axillary vein.","C":"Left jugular and subclavian lymphatic trunks can drain into the thoracic duct.","D":"Most of the right upper thoracic quadrant drains to the right lymphatic system.","E":"The duct generally courses posterior to the esophagus before turning left in the superior thorax."},"references":[{"title":"NCBI Bookshelf — Anatomy, Thorax: Thoracic Duct","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK513227/"}],"review":"independently_researched_again_2026_10_19","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"37948f69da86e60515ce8c3814d811f85d8977f4c667b9e2ff06c74c2069918b"},"q-addeef87a59e":{"correct":"B","answerText":"Intercostal artery between vein and nerve","explanation":"In the main intercostal neurovascular bundle, superior-to-inferior order is vein, artery, nerve (V-A-N), lying between internal and innermost intercostal muscles.","optionReasons":{"A":"The vein is above the artery; this wording does not describe the standard plane.","B":"The artery is between the vein and the nerve.","C":"Transversus thoracis lies deep to the internal intercostal layer.","D":"The bundle lies between internal and innermost, not external and internal, intercostals.","E":"Only option B is correct."},"references":[{"title":"NCBI Bookshelf — Anatomy, Thorax: Intercostal Nerves","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK538238/"}],"review":"independently_researched_again_2026_10_19","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"5421b6f9ab4235764b2d8d6625c7c9e576601fa9a9a453ca8f4e07dde61414f1"},"q-2f3cade2c9d9":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Intercostal artery between vein and nerve","explanation":"The neurovascular bundle is arranged vein, artery, nerve from above downward in the plane between internal and innermost intercostal muscles.","optionReasons":{"A":"The bundle is deeper than the internal intercostals, not between internal and external layers.","B":"Transversus thoracis does not run between external and internal intercostals.","C":"The artery lies between the intercostal vein and nerve.","D":"The artery is not more superficial than the vein in the standard neurovascular arrangement.","E":"Only C is the usual anatomical relationship."},"references":[{"title":"NCBI Bookshelf — Anatomy, Thorax: Intercostal Nerves","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK538238/"}],"review":"independently_researched_again_2026_10_19","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"1adea454afd7ac7f43b1fe0e632fb9bea6fa499d625c94d68fdb79f4244a1555"},"q-705fbf27e742":{"correct":"E","answerText":"None of the above","explanation":"Arterial walls contain smooth muscle in tunica media, not skeletal muscle. The question explicitly says skeletal muscle; none of the listed tunics is primarily composed of it.","optionReasons":{"A":"Tunica intima is endothelium plus connective tissue, not skeletal muscle.","B":"Tunica media contains smooth—not skeletal—muscle.","C":"Tunica externa is chiefly collagenous connective tissue.","D":"None of the three is primarily skeletal muscle.","E":"Correct: none of the listed layers is primarily skeletal muscle."},"references":[{"title":"OpenStax — Structure and Function of Blood Vessels","url":"https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/20-1-structure-and-function-of-blood-vessels"}],"review":"independently_researched_again_2026_10_19","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"9f8a6744fdc1220f514be48ab56ca4bc4e384cc62995abb1bc54adf6257bcd32"},"q-ba0eb5ff108a":{"correct":"E","answerText":"None of the above","explanation":"Arteries possess smooth muscle cells in their tunica media. None of their three tunics is primarily composed of skeletal muscle.","optionReasons":{"A":"The intima contains endothelium, not skeletal muscle.","B":"The media contains smooth muscle, not skeletal muscle.","C":"The externa is mainly fibrous connective tissue.","D":"All three are not skeletal muscle layers.","E":"Correct: none is primarily skeletal muscle."},"references":[{"title":"OpenStax — Structure and Function of Blood Vessels","url":"https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/20-1-structure-and-function-of-blood-vessels"}],"review":"independently_researched_again_2026_10_19","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"9f8a6744fdc1220f514be48ab56ca4bc4e384cc62995abb1bc54adf6257bcd32"},"q-7638ba36f12f":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Tunica intima","explanation":"The luminal surface of a blood vessel is lined by a simple squamous endothelial cell layer forming part of tunica intima.","optionReasons":{"A":"The endothelial lining forms the innermost tunica intima.","B":"Tunica media is the muscular middle layer.","C":"Tunica externa is the outer connective-tissue layer.","D":"Endothelium does not constitute all three tunics.","E":"Endothelium does exist in the intima, so none is false."},"references":[{"title":"OpenStax — Structure and Function of Blood Vessels","url":"https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/20-1-structure-and-function-of-blood-vessels"}],"review":"independently_researched_again_2026_10_19","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"0058209d8cb4f57e14d0f86900cf37c87aa7e93b08543866b082c0dfc670dec5"},"q-a42147cc1434":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Tunica externa","explanation":"Tunica externa (adventitia) is principally collagenous and elastic connective tissue and supports the vessel in surrounding tissues.","optionReasons":{"A":"Intima contains endothelium and a thin subendothelial connective layer, but is not the primarily fibrous outer tunic.","B":"Media is predominantly smooth muscle and elastic tissue.","C":"Externa is the principal collagenous connective-tissue sheath.","D":"The intima and media have different principal components.","E":"The externa is the layer requested."},"references":[{"title":"OpenStax — Structure and Function of Blood Vessels","url":"https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/20-1-structure-and-function-of-blood-vessels"}],"review":"independently_researched_again_2026_10_19","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"ca4d9819ac9ef905eecb206ba39b5173600ba8a759c29034d1aecb2a31b8e406"},"q-1b1b7d81e704":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Tunica externa","explanation":"Tunica adventitia is the alternate anatomical name for the outer vessel wall layer, tunica externa.","optionReasons":{"A":"Intima is the innermost layer, not adventitia.","B":"Media is the middle muscle-containing layer.","C":"Externa is synonymous with adventitia.","D":"Only the outer tunic has this alternate name.","E":"The tunica externa is explicitly the adventitia."},"references":[{"title":"OpenStax — Structure and Function of Blood Vessels","url":"https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/20-1-structure-and-function-of-blood-vessels"}],"review":"independently_researched_again_2026_10_19","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"cb90ff606d90a4d530da524e0aa357ea38663585e6122e334fc87cd1024f338b"},"q-31d3518bae7b":{"correct":"D","answerText":"Vagal trunks","explanation":"Both vagal trunks accompany the esophagus through its diaphragmatic hiatus at T10. Esophageal branches of the left gastric vessels and lymphatics can also accompany it.","optionReasons":{"A":"Right phrenic branches accompany the IVC at its separate caval opening.","B":"Left phrenic nerve does not travel through the esophageal hiatus.","C":"The usual accompanying artery is from the LEFT gastric artery, not right gastric.","D":"Anterior and posterior vagal trunks accompany the esophagus.","E":"Hemiazygos uses a separate route near the left crus/aortic region."},"references":[{"title":"NCBI Bookshelf — Anatomy, Thorax: Diaphragm","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519558/"}],"review":"independently_researched_again_2026_10_19","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"244911747e2bfccb16c14367fd0753c05dccdc84b0b16afc203e95405a2c7087"},"q-1db59679ab64":{"correct":"D","answerText":"The right phrenic nerve accompanies it","explanation":"The caval opening in the central tendon transmits the inferior vena cava and terminal branches of the right phrenic nerve.","optionReasons":{"A":"The caval opening is right of the midline, not left.","B":"Left phrenic nerve does not accompany the IVC through the caval opening.","C":"The standard surface location is near the right 6th costal cartilage, not the 8th.","D":"Right phrenic nerve branches accompany the IVC at T8.","E":"The caval opening is in the central tendon, not a muscular gap."},"references":[{"title":"NCBI Bookshelf — Anatomy, Thorax: Diaphragm","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519558/"}],"review":"independently_researched_again_2026_10_19","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"92114043b945ee0b84f78b533ba70e1c4edafebe7f3fe9d8b8c1f9da6d3e4a78"},"q-aa317557fcd3":{"correct":"D","answerText":"The right phrenic nerve accompanies it","explanation":"The IVC traverses the central tendon through the caval opening at T8; right phrenic nerve branches accompany it.","optionReasons":{"A":"The vena caval hiatus is right-sided.","B":"It is the right, not left, phrenic nerve that contributes accompanying branches.","C":"It is typically at the right 6th costal cartilage, not the 8th.","D":"Right phrenic nerve branches may accompany the IVC.","E":"The opening is in the central tendon rather than between muscle layers."},"references":[{"title":"NCBI Bookshelf — Anatomy, Thorax: Diaphragm","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519558/"}],"review":"independently_researched_again_2026_10_19","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"d3ac53900c97c520cc62b0af33475c0cce62d101b619d19268d80d8a4067f851"},"q-942d8caca050":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Relax bronchial smooth muscle","explanation":"Salbutamol and terbutaline are beta-2 adrenergic agonists that raise intracellular cAMP and relax bronchial smooth muscle, causing bronchodilation.","optionReasons":{"A":"Beta-2 agonists cause bronchial smooth muscle relaxation.","B":"Increasing cardiac contractility is primarily a beta-1 receptor effect.","C":"Beta-2 agonists can produce tachycardia, not reliably reduce the heart rate.","D":"Beta-2 receptors generally mediate smooth-muscle relaxation, not vasoconstriction.","E":"Activation increases cAMP rather than decreasing it."},"references":[{"title":"NCBI Bookshelf — Beta2-Agonists","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK542249/"}],"review":"independently_researched_again_2026_10_19","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"9a4b49670a27b28a2e982084cf4ee909f6c147ecd2f665c822e1376089e63f2a"},"q-01db0ad47860":{"correct":"D","answerText":"Gynecomastia","explanation":"ACE inhibitors commonly cause dry cough, hypotension, hyperkalemia, and sometimes angioedema; gynecomastia is not a characteristic ACE-inhibitor adverse effect.","optionReasons":{"A":"ACE inhibitors may cause a dry bradykinin-related cough.","B":"Reducing angiotensin II can cause hypotension.","C":"Reduced aldosterone can raise serum potassium.","D":"Gynecomastia is not a typical ACE inhibitor adverse effect; consider other agents such as spironolactone.","E":"Angioedema is a recognized, potentially life-threatening ACE-inhibitor adverse effect."},"references":[{"title":"NCBI Bookshelf — Angiotensin-Converting Enzyme Inhibitors","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK431051/"}],"review":"independently_researched_again_2026_10_19","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"6a20f338213e30dd29586a594a4cd8121cdd603024d56af60c25827788719802"},"q-d1abee7556d5":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Glomerular filtration","explanation":"Aminoglycosides are predominantly excreted unchanged in urine by glomerular filtration. Renal dysfunction reduces clearance and raises accumulation and toxicity risk.","optionReasons":{"A":"Aminoglycosides are minimally metabolized.","B":"Tubular secretion is not the dominant elimination mechanism.","C":"Glomerular filtration is the principal route of systemic aminoglycoside elimination.","D":"Biliary secretion is not the main clearance pathway.","E":"Glomerular filtration is a listed and correct route."},"references":[{"title":"NCBI Bookshelf — Gentamicin","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557550/"}],"review":"independently_researched_again_2026_10_19","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"27ffbed29495ce678fb7f2508d1047ded39c93989006cb68cfba55a8b864f8a3"},"q-dcae769da0bd":{"correct":"D","answerText":"Definitive host","explanation":"Sexual reproduction of Plasmodium occurs in the female Anopheles mosquito; the mosquito is therefore the definitive host, while humans are intermediate hosts for the parasite.","optionReasons":{"A":"Transmission is biological with parasite development, not mechanical carriage alone.","B":"It is not merely a passive transport host.","C":"Compromised host is not the parasitological host category.","D":"Plasmodium sexual reproduction occurs in the mosquito; therefore it is definitive.","E":"Humans, where asexual multiplication occurs, are the intermediate host."},"references":[{"title":"CDC DPDx — Malaria Life Cycle","url":"https://www.cdc.gov/dpdx/malaria/"}],"review":"independently_researched_again_2026_10_19","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"041b670ddf547590f2f990010adea11bcd15c8d2a6bce695e8c2cc5e1b4f8b3a"},"q-bf0d0db563db":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Carcinoma of the urinary bladder","explanation":"Long-standing Schistosoma haematobium infection is associated with urinary bladder malignancy, particularly squamous cell carcinoma, through chronic granulomatous inflammation.","optionReasons":{"A":"Esophageal varices are more characteristic of hepatosplenic portal hypertension due to intestinal schistosomiasis.","B":"Ascites relates more directly to portal hypertension than isolated urinary schistosomiasis.","C":"Bladder carcinoma is a well-established late complication of urinary S. haematobium infection.","D":"Pulmonary hypertension is linked to hepatosplenic/pulmonary schistosomiasis rather than the characteristic urogenital complication.","E":"Cor pulmonale is not the classic late complication of isolated urinary schistosomiasis."},"references":[{"title":"Schistosoma haematobium and Bladder Squamous Cell Carcinoma — PMC","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8845255/"}],"review":"independently_researched_again_2026_10_19","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"9c82f4e744dfe89acc2c157e241aec7502d0be16255965826181402c1873d249"},"q-9c569c9cf3a0":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Chorionic villus sampling","explanation":"CVS provides chorionic tissue DNA for prenatal molecular testing around 10–13 weeks, earlier than conventional second-trimester amniocentesis or fetal blood sampling.","optionReasons":{"A":"Fetoscopy is usually a later and more invasive procedure.","B":"Amniocentesis is usually performed at or after about 15 weeks.","C":"CVS can provide placental genetic material at 10–13 weeks.","D":"Percutaneous umbilical blood sampling is usually carried out later.","E":"Fetal tissue biopsy is invasive and not ordinarily the earliest routine test."},"references":[{"title":"ACOG — Prenatal Genetic Diagnostic Tests","url":"https://www.acog.org/womens-health/faqs/prenatal-genetic-diagnostic-tests"}],"review":"independently_researched_again_2026_10_19","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"472089d154b78c7d9d3341b24cb3e15ef0a1b1246fabf0822dc605b2147be2f9"},"q-7a359635e68a":{"correct":"E","answerText":"Bunyavirus","explanation":"Bunyaviruses have a segmented single-stranded RNA genome. Parvovirus, papovavirus (historical group), hepadnavirus and adenovirus are DNA viruses.","optionReasons":{"A":"Parvoviruses have single-stranded DNA genomes.","B":"Papovavirus is an older grouping of DNA viruses such as papillomaviruses and polyomaviruses.","C":"Hepadnaviruses have partially double-stranded DNA genomes.","D":"Adenoviruses have double-stranded DNA genomes.","E":"Bunyaviruses are RNA viruses, so this is the exception."},"references":[{"title":"NCBI Bookshelf — Bunyaviruses","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK8004/"}],"review":"independently_researched_again_2026_10_19","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"31dcaa87354c9cb3f29fac0f0f2f0d15e2c728fcc11a82fe333a50f412989c46"},"q-2cceb8c1ea1c":{"correct":"B","answerText":"teres major.","explanation":"The rotator cuff is formed by supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor and subscapularis (SITS). Teres major lies outside this tendon cuff.","optionReasons":{"A":"Teres minor contributes to external rotation and is one of the four cuff muscles.","B":"Teres major adducts and internally rotates the arm but is not a rotator cuff muscle.","C":"Supraspinatus is part of the cuff and initiates arm abduction.","D":"Infraspinatus is part of the cuff and externally rotates the shoulder.","E":"Subscapularis is part of the cuff and internally rotates the shoulder."},"references":[{"title":"StatPearls — Anatomy, Rotator Cuff","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK441844/"}],"review":"independent_reference_review_batch2","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"b351d91561499a5c593f510a59f561012c2188ebec954672fe995bd1f5f4c023","verificationClass":"textbook_anatomy_or_basic_science_reference_checked"},"q-875a8821d91b":{"correct":"E","answerText":"Tibialis anterior and tibialis posterior","explanation":"Tibialis anterior and tibialis posterior are the principal foot invertors, although other muscles can assist.","optionReasons":{"A":"Fibularis (peroneal) longus and brevis primarily evert the foot.","B":"The long toe flexors chiefly flex the toes and assist plantarflexion; they are not the main invertor pair.","C":"The long toe extensors chiefly extend toes and assist dorsiflexion, not principal inversion.","D":"Gastrocnemius and soleus chiefly plantarflex the ankle.","E":"Both tibialis muscles invert the foot and support the medial arch."},"references":[{"title":"StatPearls — Tibialis Anterior","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK513304/"}],"review":"independent_reference_review_batch2","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"5ade5e66fd3606333f976a0e1fd151a4c261bd67ae19b07efec1ca624df9fba2","verificationClass":"textbook_anatomy_or_basic_science_reference_checked"},"q-a5fe08ea29d6":{"correct":"D","answerText":"Osteoid","explanation":"Osteoid is the unmineralized collagen-rich organic matrix secreted by osteoblasts prior to mineral deposition.","optionReasons":{"A":"An osteoclast is a bone-resorbing cell, not a matrix.","B":"An osteoblast produces osteoid but is a cell, not the matrix itself.","C":"An osteon is the structural unit of compact bone.","D":"Osteoid is unmineralized bone matrix.","E":"There is a specific matching option: osteoid."},"references":[{"title":"StatPearls — Bone Ossification","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK539718/"}],"review":"independent_reference_review_batch2","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"8df1c300c7f94594935c7465d2172e50b387323cf9e62c037934f844aefec2ac","verificationClass":"textbook_anatomy_or_basic_science_reference_checked"},"q-cd1c3a940746":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Aorta.","explanation":"The ovarian artery usually arises directly from the abdominal aorta, inferior to the renal arteries, and travels through the suspensory ligament of the ovary.","optionReasons":{"A":"The common iliac artery is not the usual origin of the ovarian artery.","B":"The internal iliac gives rise to the uterine artery, rather than being the usual ovarian-artery origin.","C":"The abdominal aorta gives rise directly to the ovarian artery.","D":"Hypogastric artery is another name for internal iliac artery, not the direct origin here.","E":"Sacral arteries supply sacral and adjacent pelvic structures, not the ovary as its principal named artery."},"references":[{"title":"StatPearls — Bony Pelvis: Bones","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519524/"}],"review":"independent_reference_review_batch2","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"ba5d208235d4ac7f1c6690c4c5587679e089c00f1784b3fecae4331f2a59f469","verificationClass":"textbook_anatomy_or_basic_science_reference_checked"},"q-257bae13c075":{"correct":"B","answerText":"Perineurium","explanation":"A peripheral nerve fascicle (bundle of nerve fibers) is enclosed by perineurium; endoneurium surrounds individual axons, and epineurium surrounds the entire nerve.","optionReasons":{"A":"“Neurium” alone is not a standard distinct connective-tissue layer in this three-layer scheme.","B":"Perineurium encloses a fascicle of nerve fibers.","C":"Epineurium envelops the whole peripheral nerve.","D":"Endoneurium invests an individual axon/nerve fiber within a fascicle.","E":"Perineurium is explicitly the correct fascicular sheath."},"references":[{"title":"OpenStax — Peripheral Nervous System","url":"https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/13-4-the-peripheral-nervous-system"}],"review":"independent_reference_review_batch2","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"d667a4bd356864174b6dd4d3f43156843fbed40c9a95a48232cacba3b142cee7","verificationClass":"textbook_anatomy_or_basic_science_reference_checked"},"q-c14e6a3c300c":{"correct":"E","answerText":"Muscular artery","explanation":"The brachial artery is a distributing muscular artery, with a tunica media rich in smooth muscle.","optionReasons":{"A":"Capillaries are microscopic exchange vessels, not the brachial artery.","B":"Arterioles are small resistance vessels downstream of muscular arteries.","C":"Venules are part of the venous microcirculation.","D":"Elastic arteries include large conducting arteries, such as the aorta.","E":"The brachial artery is a medium-sized muscular artery."},"references":[{"title":"StatPearls — Histology, Blood Vascular System","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK553217/"}],"review":"independent_reference_review_batch2","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"ffa73fc83281f30e7da52671d641bb4efadcc3d0e19895677e97d7cae5513623","verificationClass":"textbook_anatomy_or_basic_science_reference_checked"},"q-2931bd0e51e3":{"correct":"D","answerText":"syndesmoses, gomphoses, sutures","explanation":"Fibrous joints are structurally classified as sutures, syndesmoses, and gomphoses.","optionReasons":{"A":"Plane, hinge, pivot, saddle and condyloid are synovial joint classes.","B":"Condyloid and ball-and-socket are synovial; symphysis is cartilaginous.","C":"Synchondrosis is cartilaginous, and the movement joints listed are synovial.","D":"Syndesmosis, gomphosis and suture are all fibrous joint categories.","E":"Synchondrosis and symphysis are the two types of cartilaginous joint."},"references":[{"title":"OpenStax — Fibrous Joints","url":"https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/9-2-fibrous-joints"}],"review":"independent_reference_review_batch2","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"57fe5276dc069956591377bc04afdca9d407a7103b71cb7b5f859faf2ae7cd7b","verificationClass":"textbook_anatomy_or_basic_science_reference_checked"},"q-4463934199ee":{"correct":"B","answerText":"Ilium, ischium, pubis, sacrum & coccyx.","explanation":"The bony pelvis consists of the paired hip bones (each ilium, ischium and pubis), sacrum, and coccyx.","optionReasons":{"A":"Trochanters and the hip socket are landmarks rather than additional named pelvic bones.","B":"This includes the three fused bones on each side plus sacrum and coccyx.","C":"Ilium, ischium and pubis make up the hip bones but omit the sacrum and coccyx.","D":"This omits the coccyx from the complete bony pelvis.","E":"Trochanters belong to the femur, not the pelvis."},"references":[{"title":"StatPearls — Anatomy, Pelvis","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482258/"}],"review":"independent_reference_review_batch2","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"8e80e109f0ef30d3d096e4b433a3dc90602ff555e4005421f08dd38b18e33cce","verificationClass":"textbook_anatomy_or_basic_science_reference_checked"},"q-248f1975f7df":{"correct":"D","answerText":"Left ventricle","explanation":"Phrenic sensory fibers supply the central diaphragm, diaphragmatic peritoneum, mediastinal pleura, and fibrous/parietal pericardium. The ventricular myocardium is not somatically supplied by the phrenic nerve.","optionReasons":{"A":"The phrenic supplies central diaphragmatic somatic sensation.","B":"Mediastinal pleural sensation travels through the phrenic nerve.","C":"The central diaphragmatic peritoneum receives phrenic sensation; peripheral peritoneum has other supply.","D":"The left ventricular myocardium is not a phrenic sensory territory.","E":"The fibrous/parietal pericardium receives sensory supply from the phrenic."},"references":[{"title":"StatPearls — Anatomy, Thorax: Phrenic Nerves","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK513325/"}],"review":"independent_reference_review_batch2","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"3bdd22f38eb190a11555d26cdf1ceaa9754f1bf7b979c1a0725e5b4778855f96","verificationClass":"textbook_anatomy_or_basic_science_reference_checked"},"q-f95f6f2b831f":{"correct":"A","answerText":"C. difficile","explanation":"Toxigenic Clostridioides difficile causes antibiotic-associated colitis and may produce pseudomembranous colitis.","optionReasons":{"A":"Clostridioides difficile is classically associated with pseudomembranous colitis.","B":"Clostridium perfringens usually causes food poisoning or gas gangrene.","C":"Clostridium septicum is associated with invasive soft-tissue infections and bacteremia.","D":"Clostridium botulinum causes botulism from neurotoxin.","E":"Clostridium tetani causes tetanus from neurotoxin."},"references":[{"title":"CDC — C. diff infection","url":"https://www.cdc.gov/c-diff/about/index.html"}],"review":"independent_reference_review_batch2","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"fea0436b11b7612c65d0b2643f26c50e3d78b5ca463ebdb9e9b80581d942eb93","verificationClass":"textbook_anatomy_or_basic_science_reference_checked"},"q-ad634f908439":{"correct":"C","answerText":"neutralizing secretory IgA in the mucus.","explanation":"Secretory IgA in mucus binds pathogens and their toxins, impeding epithelial adherence and colonization while preserving mucosal barrier function.","optionReasons":{"A":"Cytotoxic T cells can kill infected cells, but are not the principal luminal neutralizing antibody system.","B":"Macrophages ordinarily function in tissues, not as the primary defense floating in intestinal lumen.","C":"Secretory IgA neutralizes pathogens within mucus before epithelial invasion.","D":"Secretory IgA primarily mediates immune exclusion, not classic opsonization on M cells.","E":"Immune tolerance restrains responses to harmless antigens; it does not chiefly eliminate pathogens."},"references":[{"title":"Immunobiology — The Mucosal Immune System","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK27169/"}],"review":"independent_reference_review_batch2","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"25425a44b1439e69af810fb674a98b5a1ebdb081f8cc270a1b37529fa09f3207","verificationClass":"textbook_anatomy_or_basic_science_reference_checked"},"q-31be9dc2811a":{"correct":"D","answerText":"provide the microenvironment for maturation of T and B cells.","explanation":"Bone marrow and thymus are primary lymphoid organs where B and T cells develop and mature, respectively.","optionReasons":{"A":"Antigen encounters and clonal responses mostly take place in secondary lymphoid organs.","B":"Active antibody secretion is largely by plasma cells in secondary lymphoid tissues and effector sites.","C":"Blood-borne antigen filtration is a main function of the spleen.","D":"Primary lymphoid organs provide developmental environments for B and T lymphocytes.","E":"Mucosal antigen surveillance is a feature of MALT, a secondary lymphoid compartment."},"references":[{"title":"Immunobiology — Components of the Immune System","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK27092/"}],"review":"independent_reference_review_batch2","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"0d995844efeee6b46f06d29be0e80f30859a29f5a558bef5252d072714e10e3c","verificationClass":"textbook_anatomy_or_basic_science_reference_checked"},"q-a45b7aaf10cf":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Catalase","explanation":"Catalase decomposes hydrogen peroxide into water and oxygen; the released oxygen produces visible bubbling.","optionReasons":{"A":"The catalase reaction generates oxygen bubbles on addition of hydrogen peroxide.","B":"Coagulase is detected by clotting plasma, not this bubbling reaction.","C":"Bile-esculin hydrolysis darkens the medium.","D":"Bacitracin is used as an antimicrobial susceptibility disk, not a bubble-producing agent.","E":"Optochin is a susceptibility disk used in pneumococcal identification."},"references":[{"title":"Medical Microbiology — Staphylococcus","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK8448/"}],"review":"independent_reference_review_batch2","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"d302272bcab4ce6e674fbf5d9ac134293f102765bf6a04d83bc0b42a5857a90e","verificationClass":"textbook_anatomy_or_basic_science_reference_checked"},"q-ffcf7fcd9192":{"correct":"E","answerText":"P. vulgaris","explanation":"Proteus vulgaris is strongly motile and characteristically produces spreading concentric swarms over suitable solid media.","optionReasons":{"A":"Escherichia coli may be motile, but it does not characteristically form Proteus-type spreading swarms.","B":"Shigella species are generally nonmotile.","C":"Klebsiella pneumoniae is nonmotile and typically forms mucoid colonies.","D":"Salmonella is commonly motile but not characteristically a Proteus-type swarmer.","E":"Proteus vulgaris exhibits flagella-driven swarming on agar."},"references":[{"title":"Journal of Medical Microbiology — Proteus Swarming Motility","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/379339/"}],"review":"independent_reference_review_batch2","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"6e0310173428f9996c06d8734923848f268d7a1165acfed54c634588068b5cea","verificationClass":"textbook_anatomy_or_basic_science_reference_checked"},"q-e6f82ad07a97":{"correct":"A","answerText":"secretory IgA.","explanation":"Secretory IgA is the major immunoglobulin mediating neutralization and immune exclusion at the intestinal mucosa.","optionReasons":{"A":"Secretory IgA coats and neutralizes pathogens in gut mucus before epithelial entry.","B":"Serum IgA is predominantly circulating monomer, not the major luminal barrier antibody.","C":"Serum IgD is not the principal mucosal neutralizing antibody.","D":"Serum IgG can protect tissues but is not the dominant luminal antibody.","E":"Membrane IgM is a B-cell receptor rather than the chief gut-mucus antibody."},"references":[{"title":"Immunobiology — The Mucosal Immune System","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK27169/"}],"review":"independent_reference_review_batch2","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"b5dc2fa05d90b1b255120174a58dda7850e9e7bc2eef75f3854cf37abd182d4b","verificationClass":"textbook_anatomy_or_basic_science_reference_checked"},"q-6fb2d71f472c":{"correct":"E","answerText":"vancomycin","explanation":"Vancomycin is too bulky to cross the intact Gram-negative outer membrane, explaining the intrinsic resistance of most Gram-negative organisms.","optionReasons":{"A":"Tetracyclines can access susceptible Gram-negative bacteria via uptake pathways.","B":"Aminoglycosides can enter and treat selected aerobic Gram-negative bacteria.","C":"Many quinolones penetrate the Gram-negative envelope and act on topoisomerases.","D":"Rifampicin penetration is variable and often limited, but vancomycin is the canonical excluded glycopeptide.","E":"Vancomycin does not penetrate the Gram-negative outer membrane effectively."},"references":[{"title":"Medical Microbiology — Antimicrobial Chemotherapy","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK7986/"}],"review":"independent_reference_review_batch2","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"cb4f73a6989eb801eb37f2bf950ebc1255a8bdadd672b12aea30b9e8e984db7f","verificationClass":"textbook_anatomy_or_basic_science_reference_checked"},"q-6b7031d00783":{"correct":"A","answerText":"allelic exclusion.","explanation":"Allelic exclusion ensures that one productive immunoglobulin heavy-chain allele is generally expressed in a B-cell clone, contributing to single antigen specificity.","optionReasons":{"A":"Allelic exclusion limits expression to one immunoglobulin heavy-chain allele in an individual B cell.","B":"Codominant expression would permit both alleles to be expressed.","C":"Isotypic exclusion is not the name for restricting heavy-chain allele expression.","D":"Nonproductive rearrangement is one possible rearrangement outcome, not the regulatory phenomenon.","E":"“Survival of the fittest” is not a term for B-cell receptor allelic restriction."},"references":[{"title":"Immunobiology — B-cell Receptor Gene Rearrangement","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK27123/"}],"review":"independent_reference_review_batch2","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"0497d6da0186752c3a85810d0dadbbc2c97e7f334118f68b96766b80a1c96ab0","verificationClass":"textbook_anatomy_or_basic_science_reference_checked"},"q-0b1e46e87260":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Tissue preference of a virus","explanation":"Viral tropism is the preference of a virus for particular host tissues or cell types, determined by entry receptors and intracellular permissiveness.","optionReasons":{"A":"Tissue or cellular preference is the definition of viral tropism.","B":"Viral replication efficiency can influence tropism but does not itself define the term.","C":"Loss of virulence factors refers to attenuation, not tropism.","D":"Antiviral immune response is the host reaction to infection.","E":"Population-level viral disease frequency is epidemiology, not tropism."},"references":[{"title":"Medical Microbiology — Viral Pathogenesis","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK8149/"}],"review":"independent_reference_review_batch2","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"2834be91487335000647d7ca8085f25139b2c966def61c54923c0cf6cc3af1a1","verificationClass":"textbook_anatomy_or_basic_science_reference_checked"},"q-12f5230bf715":{"correct":"D","answerText":"Epstein-Barr virus (EBV)","explanation":"Epstein–Barr virus is etiologically associated with endemic Burkitt lymphoma, some Hodgkin lymphomas, and nasopharyngeal carcinoma.","optionReasons":{"A":"HSV-1 usually causes orolabial herpes and is not the common oncogenic link across these three cancers.","B":"HSV-2 primarily causes genital herpes and is not the shared oncogenic agent.","C":"VZV causes varicella and herpes zoster, not the shared association.","D":"EBV is the herpesvirus consistently associated with all three conditions.","E":"CMV is an opportunistic pathogen but is not the classic shared cause of these malignancies."},"references":[{"title":"IARC — Epstein–Barr Virus and Cancer","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK304353/"}],"review":"independent_reference_review_batch2","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"1e1791433c96d9fb880ceb4c4daf99f12fe7c5f11a4af86c552ad1a8e293fa3c","verificationClass":"textbook_anatomy_or_basic_science_reference_checked"},"q-e2d7e3c29618":{"correct":"D","answerText":"themselves.","explanation":"Activated T cells can secrete IL-2 and express the high-affinity IL-2 receptor; this autocrine signaling supports antigen-driven T-cell proliferation.","optionReasons":{"A":"B cells are not the principal IL-2 source driving initial expansion of activated T cells.","B":"Dendritic cells present antigen and costimulation but are not the standard autocrine IL-2 source.","C":"Macrophages supply other activating cytokines, not the principal T-cell autocrine IL-2 source.","D":"The activated T cells themselves make IL-2 that promotes clonal expansion.","E":"Any antigen-presenting cell is not interchangeable with T-cell-derived IL-2 in this mechanism."},"references":[{"title":"Immunobiology — T-cell Activation","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK27146/"}],"review":"independent_reference_review_batch2","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"e684294a02f4924284d8baa56c3f5ee78434499a3252a33c04277bb80b7aa6f3","verificationClass":"textbook_anatomy_or_basic_science_reference_checked"},"q-85d751fe9e7c":{"correct":"B","answerText":"Thymus","explanation":"The thymic supporting framework contains thymic epithelial reticular cells, unlike the collagenous reticular networks of most other lymphoid organs.","optionReasons":{"A":"Splenic stroma contains connective-tissue reticular fibers and various immune cells.","B":"The thymus has an epithelial-reticular cell scaffold that supports maturing T cells.","C":"Bone marrow has stromal reticular and hematopoietic cells, not a thymic epithelioreticular framework.","D":"Lymph nodes are supported by reticular connective tissue rather than thymic epithelial reticular cells.","E":"The thymus provides the matching histology."},"references":[{"title":"PubMed — Histophysiology of Thymic Epithelial Cells","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/6751281/"}],"review":"independent_reference_review_batch2","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"5178f1341d4291b944495ed1b73a09634cf5258e648564b9b5b5f3842a988418","verificationClass":"textbook_anatomy_or_basic_science_reference_checked"},"q-fc54f6b477a5":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Dura mater","explanation":"Dura mater is a tough outer meninx composed mainly of dense collagenous connective tissue; arachnoid and pia are thinner coverings.","optionReasons":{"A":"Dura mater is the outer dense fibrous meninx.","B":"Arachnoid is a thin membrane with trabeculae, not the dense fibrous outer meninx.","C":"Pia is a delicate vascular inner membrane that closely follows the brain surface.","D":"Only dura, not both dura and arachnoid, fits this classic histological description.","E":"The three meningeal layers are histologically distinct."},"references":[{"title":"StatPearls — Anatomy, Dura Mater","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK545301/"}],"review":"independent_reference_review_batch2","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"3ac305d67280b22f24c6acf7565ad26d71986332ba329fb5e4af4662d5258f6e","verificationClass":"textbook_anatomy_or_basic_science_reference_checked"},"q-c06648805a6a":{"correct":"A","answerText":"H. nana","explanation":"Hymenolepis nana eggs contain a six-hooked oncosphere and polar filaments; this distinguishes them from most other cestode eggs.","optionReasons":{"A":"H. nana eggs have an oncosphere with six hooks and distinctive polar filaments.","B":"Taenia solium eggs have a radially striated embryophore, not the polar filaments described.","C":"Taenia saginata eggs resemble T. solium eggs, without these polar filaments.","D":"Echinococcus granulosus eggs are taeniid-type, not characteristic H. nana eggs.","E":"Diphyllobothrium latum eggs are operculated and lack this polar-filament pattern."},"references":[{"title":"Clinical Microbiology Guidance — Gastrointestinal Parasites","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5740970/"}],"review":"independent_reference_review_batch2","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"246bb8609e7351a0c9037f9c06f4d1d51474b65a4d44e8d03234bf03a4fa4859","verificationClass":"textbook_anatomy_or_basic_science_reference_checked"},"q-d73a6fd0948e":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Increased plasma volume.","explanation":"In pregnancy, plasma volume rises proportionately more than red cell mass, lowering measured hemoglobin concentration through hemodilution.","optionReasons":{"A":"Iron deficiency can coexist with pregnancy but does not explain physiological hemodilution in every woman.","B":"Normal physiological pregnancy anemia is not caused by blood loss into the placenta.","C":"Increased plasma volume dilutes circulating hemoglobin concentration.","D":"Increased cardiac output does not cause the routine rise in red cell destruction proposed here.","E":"Reduced reticulocytosis is not the mechanism of physiological dilutional anemia."},"references":[{"title":"StatPearls — Physiology, Maternal Changes","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK539766/"}],"review":"independent_reference_review_batch2","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"2cb8957954e1bc74a3d676e5d55bae9e512f511b06e26f3a987f26395db342ed","verificationClass":"textbook_anatomy_or_basic_science_reference_checked"},"q-76f1181f3e1d":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Peripheral resistance.","explanation":"Systemic vascular resistance generally decreases during pregnancy despite increased plasma volume, stroke volume and glomerular filtration rate.","optionReasons":{"A":"GFR rises in normal pregnancy.","B":"Stroke volume and cardiac output usually increase.","C":"Systemic peripheral resistance falls rather than rises.","D":"Maternal plasma volume increases substantially.","E":"Total leukocyte count commonly rises in pregnancy."},"references":[{"title":"StatPearls — Physiology, Pregnancy","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559304/"}],"review":"independent_reference_review_batch2","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"b8b40c878de53a05adc2495288a6e51010cca2dd587c7c01051c80a0d53ecb3f","verificationClass":"textbook_anatomy_or_basic_science_reference_checked"},"q-6703a1b23066":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Increased progesterone production.","explanation":"Progesterone enhances ventilatory drive and increases tidal volume during pregnancy, producing physiological mild hyperventilation and lower PaCO2.","optionReasons":{"A":"Plasma bicarbonate falls as renal compensation after respiratory alkalosis; fluctuations do not initiate it.","B":"Estrogen has physiological effects, but progesterone is the dominant respiratory stimulant.","C":"Increased progesterone raises ventilatory drive.","D":"Reduced functional residual capacity is mechanical adaptation, not the primary ventilatory stimulant.","E":"Maternal PaO2 is generally maintained or mildly increased rather than reduced."},"references":[{"title":"StatPearls — Physiology, Maternal Changes","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK539766/"}],"review":"independent_reference_review_batch2","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"72bf8dae537765e4541db3851bbcd6ac686bfdf34a7dd41d093a5f48f7633320","verificationClass":"textbook_anatomy_or_basic_science_reference_checked"},"q-653004cebd59":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Folic acid.","explanation":"Folic acid before conception and in early pregnancy reduces the risk of neural tube defects during early neural tube closure.","optionReasons":{"A":"Vitamin B6 is not the standard neural-tube-defect preventive nutrient.","B":"Iron prevents or treats iron-deficiency anemia but is not the specific preventive intervention.","C":"Folic acid supplementation around conception reduces neural tube defects.","D":"Zinc deficiency affects development but zinc is not the primary proven supplement for this purpose.","E":"Magnesium is not the primary preventive supplement for neural tube closure defects."},"references":[{"title":"CDC — Neural Tube Defects","url":"https://www.cdc.gov/birth-defects/about/neural-tube-defects.html"}],"review":"independent_reference_review_batch2","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"5f8d4a1eb9020416f4523b3b4ce0ff7d301b5966ecaf3ab5b02d266bc91d40c7","verificationClass":"textbook_anatomy_or_basic_science_reference_checked"},"q-8970aacc064f":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Attitude of the fetus.","explanation":"Fetal attitude describes the relation of fetal parts to one another, especially flexion and extension of the head, trunk and limbs.","optionReasons":{"A":"Presentation identifies the fetal part entering the pelvic inlet first.","B":"Lie compares the fetal longitudinal axis with that of the mother.","C":"Attitude describes flexion and relative positions of fetal body parts.","D":"Position relates a fetal reference point to the maternal pelvis.","E":"The described relationship is fetal attitude."},"references":[{"title":"StatPearls — Normal Labor: Mechanics of Delivery","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/medgen/452283"}],"review":"independent_reference_review_batch2","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"22848b31b3c3499bad4892b330a4e7507b63df3ad6d231c89d6a34875de3dbad","verificationClass":"textbook_anatomy_or_basic_science_reference_checked"},"q-ed9776d4389b":{"correct":"B","answerText":"Resistance to acid elution.","explanation":"The Kleihauer–Betke test uses the acid resistance of fetal hemoglobin to distinguish fetal red cells from adult maternal red cells in a blood smear.","optionReasons":{"A":"Cell shape alone is not a reliable discriminator of fetal versus maternal erythrocytes.","B":"Fetal hemoglobin resists acid elution; adult HbA-rich cells become pale ghosts.","C":"Fetal cells are not defined by universal absence of Rh antigen.","D":"Fetal RBCs do not characteristically have lower hemoglobin amount sufficient for differentiation.","E":"Only the acid-elution property is reliable among the listed methods."},"references":[{"title":"StatPearls — Kleihauer-Betke Test","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430876/"}],"review":"independent_reference_review_batch2","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"28a70b092962a207506ca383646df028e62ed6631503ab11c000e3835650eb21","verificationClass":"textbook_anatomy_or_basic_science_reference_checked"},"q-e5361095e5a3":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Vitamin K","explanation":"Vitamin K is required for clotting-factor gamma-carboxylation, not erythrocyte maturation. Iron, folate and vitamin B12 are key to normal erythropoiesis.","optionReasons":{"A":"Vitamin K supports hemostasis and is not a standard red-cell maturation factor.","B":"Iron is needed for heme and hemoglobin synthesis.","C":"Vitamin B12 supports DNA synthesis in erythroid precursors.","D":"Folate supports DNA synthesis in erythroid precursors.","E":"Not all of these are required for erythrocyte maturation; vitamin K is the exception."},"references":[{"title":"OpenStax — Erythrocytes","url":"https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/18-3-erythrocytes"}],"review":"independent_reference_review_batch2","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"d5a0282638aaefdb8dd73122da5fe0cdb361d5f4bccb7a5b583175e10f4bbfec","verificationClass":"textbook_anatomy_or_basic_science_reference_checked"},"q-4190a2cddac6":{"correct":"B","answerText":"Neutrophil","explanation":"Neutrophils are the predominant circulating granulocytes and a key first-line phagocytic leukocyte in acute bacterial infection.","optionReasons":{"A":"Monocytes are circulating phagocytes but less numerous than neutrophils.","B":"Neutrophils are the principal abundant circulating phagocytes.","C":"Macrophages are differentiated tissue-resident cells, not the main circulating population.","D":"Eosinophils mainly mediate responses to parasites and allergies.","E":"Plasma cells are differentiated B cells that secrete immunoglobulin rather than phagocytose."},"references":[{"title":"StatPearls — Neutrophil","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26846/"}],"review":"independent_reference_review_batch2","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"54a9597a9297f2ba1b1c4e2f63571f1da617e7a64f18caca766db27a6f7b5e96","verificationClass":"textbook_anatomy_or_basic_science_reference_checked"},"q-1b69f9459daf":{"correct":"B","answerText":"Protects the red blood cell against oxidative stress","explanation":"Reduced glutathione protects erythrocyte proteins, hemoglobin, and membranes from reactive oxygen species; its recycling depends on NADPH.","optionReasons":{"A":"Glutathione reduces oxidative injury rather than oxidizing red cell membranes.","B":"Reduced glutathione neutralizes reactive oxygen species.","C":"Glycolysis produces most erythrocyte ATP; glutathione is chiefly an antioxidant.","D":"The pentose phosphate pathway makes NADPH used to regenerate glutathione, not vice versa.","E":"Glutathione is essential for red-cell antioxidant defense."},"references":[{"title":"StatPearls — G6PD Deficiency","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470315/"}],"review":"independent_reference_review_batch2","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"699ae44600415fb4b3262f81c84b079ac52bc05ba166bc93be1c4c44523e7205","verificationClass":"textbook_anatomy_or_basic_science_reference_checked"},"q-f1b552b771ee":{"correct":"D","answerText":"Ring sideroblasts in the marrow nucleated red cells.","explanation":"Ring sideroblasts are typical of sideroblastic disorders and reflect mitochondrial iron accumulation, not uncomplicated iron deficiency anemia.","optionReasons":{"A":"Iron deficiency classically causes microcytic, hypochromic red cells.","B":"Reduced heme synthesis results in poor hemoglobinization of erythroid precursors.","C":"Elliptical pencil cells are a common blood-film finding in iron deficiency.","D":"Ring sideroblasts suggest sideroblastic erythropoiesis rather than straightforward iron deficiency.","E":"Iron stores are depleted or absent in iron deficiency."},"references":[{"title":"StatPearls — Iron Deficiency Anemia","url":"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/hematology/anemias-caused-by-deficient-erythropoiesis/iron-deficiency-anemia"}],"review":"independent_reference_review_batch2","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"9c549593cbc687bbc8fcc62558fcafc10671ed3a747c32e429d866dd31e7bd75","verificationClass":"textbook_anatomy_or_basic_science_reference_checked"},"q-84f5c77fcd63":{"correct":"B","answerText":"The most common form is hemophilia B","explanation":"Hemophilia A (factor VIII deficiency) is far more common than hemophilia B (factor IX deficiency). Thus the statement that B is the most common is false.","optionReasons":{"A":"Hereditary factor deficiencies account for the classic forms of hemophilia.","B":"Hemophilia A is more prevalent than hemophilia B.","C":"Hemophilia B involves deficient or dysfunctional factor IX.","D":"Hemophilia A involves deficient or dysfunctional factor VIII.","E":"Both classic hemophilias affect coagulation factor activity."},"references":[{"title":"StatPearls — Hemophilia","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK551607/"}],"review":"independent_reference_review_batch2","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","questionTextHash":"c466cfbc0450c92f881b12c0a32e5f0246a44a85aecd556ef9966f36fcda5e20","verificationClass":"textbook_anatomy_or_basic_science_reference_checked"},"q-18f3bd0096aa":{"correct":"E","answerText":"Neonates","explanation":"Group B Streptococcus (Streptococcus agalactiae) is an important cause of early- and late-onset neonatal infection including sepsis and meningitis.","optionReasons":{"A":"Elderly men does not provide the best response to this question. The deciding principle is: Group B Streptococcus (Streptococcus agalactiae) is an important cause of early- and late-onset neonatal infection including sepsis and meningitis.","B":"Elderly women does not provide the best response to this question. The deciding principle is: Group B Streptococcus (Streptococcus agalactiae) is an important cause of early- and late-onset neonatal infection including sepsis and meningitis.","C":"Teenage boys does not provide the best response to this question. The deciding principle is: Group B Streptococcus (Streptococcus agalactiae) is an important cause of early- and late-onset neonatal infection including sepsis and meningitis.","D":"Teenage girls does not provide the best response to this question. The deciding principle is: Group B Streptococcus (Streptococcus agalactiae) is an important cause of early- and late-onset neonatal infection including sepsis and meningitis.","E":"Group B Streptococcus (Streptococcus agalactiae) is an important cause of early- and late-onset neonatal infection including sepsis and meningitis."},"references":[],"review":"model_reasoned_answer_draft_original_source_key_not_consulted","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","provisional":true,"questionTextHash":"65c39014f6e7fa2caf84e341be0da4eeca21f6e2172225ca936b8ae38e855705"},"q-45b6c6077402":{"correct":"E","answerText":"T cells.","explanation":"Cell-mediated adaptive immunity relies chiefly on antigen-specific T lymphocytes acting as effector cells, whereas antibodies are the main humoral effectors.","optionReasons":{"A":"antibodies. does not provide the best response to this question. The deciding principle is: Cell-mediated adaptive immunity relies chiefly on antigen-specific T lymphocytes acting as effector cells, whereas antibodies are the main humoral effectors.","B":"B cells. does not provide the best response to this question. The deciding principle is: Cell-mediated adaptive immunity relies chiefly on antigen-specific T lymphocytes acting as effector cells, whereas antibodies are the main humoral effectors.","C":"cytokines. does not provide the best response to this question. The deciding principle is: Cell-mediated adaptive immunity relies chiefly on antigen-specific T lymphocytes acting as effector cells, whereas antibodies are the main humoral effectors.","D":"macrophages does not provide the best response to this question. The deciding principle is: Cell-mediated adaptive immunity relies chiefly on antigen-specific T lymphocytes acting as effector cells, whereas antibodies are the main humoral effectors.","E":"Cell-mediated adaptive immunity relies chiefly on antigen-specific T lymphocytes acting as effector cells, whereas antibodies are the main humoral effectors."},"references":[],"review":"model_reasoned_answer_draft_original_source_key_not_consulted","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","provisional":true,"questionTextHash":"54759b5aa729a2c4d3494345a56362261256ad0b84c6c851a7839fd57c076c82"},"q-386d65093ee3":{"correct":"A","answerText":"ITAMs.","explanation":"Immunoreceptor tyrosine-based activation motifs (ITAMs) are phosphorylated after antigen-receptor engagement and initiate intracellular signaling.","optionReasons":{"A":"Immunoreceptor tyrosine-based activation motifs (ITAMs) are phosphorylated after antigen-receptor engagement and initiate intracellular signaling.","B":"ITIMs. does not provide the best response to this question. The deciding principle is: Immunoreceptor tyrosine-based activation motifs (ITAMs) are phosphorylated after antigen-receptor engagement and initiate intracellular signaling.","C":"MAPs. does not provide the best response to this question. The deciding principle is: Immunoreceptor tyrosine-based activation motifs (ITAMs) are phosphorylated after antigen-receptor engagement and initiate intracellular signaling.","D":"PTKs. does not provide the best response to this question. The deciding principle is: Immunoreceptor tyrosine-based activation motifs (ITAMs) are phosphorylated after antigen-receptor engagement and initiate intracellular signaling.","E":"syks. does not provide the best response to this question. The deciding principle is: Immunoreceptor tyrosine-based activation motifs (ITAMs) are phosphorylated after antigen-receptor engagement and initiate intracellular signaling."},"references":[],"review":"model_reasoned_answer_draft_original_source_key_not_consulted","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","provisional":true,"questionTextHash":"6efde2badc33ad12578c103672d2ca7303a9c9d73e654ee6536e8dc5990ec51e"},"q-9a8ab344d7e8":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Streptococcus pneumoniae, Hemophilus influenzae, Meningococcus","explanation":"In children beyond the neonatal period, the classic major bacterial meningitis pathogens are Streptococcus pneumoniae, Neisseria meningitidis and historically Hib; vaccination affects current proportions.","optionReasons":{"A":"Salmonella, Streptococcus Group A, Staphylococcus does not provide the best response to this question. The deciding principle is: In children beyond the neonatal period, the classic major bacterial meningitis pathogens are Streptococcus pneumoniae, Neisseria meningitidis and historically Hib; vaccination affects current proportions.","B":"E. Coli, Streptococcus pneumoniae, Hemophilus influenzae does not provide the best response to this question. The deciding principle is: In children beyond the neonatal period, the classic major bacterial meningitis pathogens are Streptococcus pneumoniae, Neisseria meningitidis and historically Hib; vaccination affects current proportions.","C":"In children beyond the neonatal period, the classic major bacterial meningitis pathogens are Streptococcus pneumoniae, Neisseria meningitidis and historically Hib; vaccination affects current proportions.","D":"Beta haemolytic Streptococcus Group B, Streptococcus pneumoniae, E. Coli does not provide the best response to this question. The deciding principle is: In children beyond the neonatal period, the classic major bacterial meningitis pathogens are Streptococcus pneumoniae, Neisseria meningitidis and historically Hib; vaccination affects current proportions."},"references":[],"review":"model_reasoned_answer_draft_original_source_key_not_consulted","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","provisional":true,"questionTextHash":"f1c29b46c57203565b4964355af834fe684a1379b03004c941da4a0314515669"},"q-80b8c02085a3":{"correct":"A","answerText":"lymphocytes","explanation":"Adoptive transfer of cellular immune responsiveness involves transferring immune lymphocytes, whereas transfer of serum antibodies provides passive humoral immunity.","optionReasons":{"A":"Adoptive transfer of cellular immune responsiveness involves transferring immune lymphocytes, whereas transfer of serum antibodies provides passive humoral immunity.","B":"serum does not provide the best response to this question. The deciding principle is: Adoptive transfer of cellular immune responsiveness involves transferring immune lymphocytes, whereas transfer of serum antibodies provides passive humoral immunity.","C":"Antibody does not provide the best response to this question. The deciding principle is: Adoptive transfer of cellular immune responsiveness involves transferring immune lymphocytes, whereas transfer of serum antibodies provides passive humoral immunity.","D":"phagocytes does not provide the best response to this question. The deciding principle is: Adoptive transfer of cellular immune responsiveness involves transferring immune lymphocytes, whereas transfer of serum antibodies provides passive humoral immunity."},"references":[],"review":"model_reasoned_answer_draft_original_source_key_not_consulted","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","provisional":true,"questionTextHash":"39e7f66b92ec6572e9617207d3b377ff3ce4bca6842e8d752bc6e072f6749a3b"},"q-d3ef45ea1286":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Pili","explanation":"Pili and fimbriae mediate bacterial adhesion; specialized conjugative pili also participate in transfer of genetic material.","optionReasons":{"A":"Pili and fimbriae mediate bacterial adhesion; specialized conjugative pili also participate in transfer of genetic material.","B":"Capsules does not provide the best response to this question. The deciding principle is: Pili and fimbriae mediate bacterial adhesion; specialized conjugative pili also participate in transfer of genetic material.","C":"Flagella does not provide the best response to this question. The deciding principle is: Pili and fimbriae mediate bacterial adhesion; specialized conjugative pili also participate in transfer of genetic material.","D":"Plasmids does not provide the best response to this question. The deciding principle is: Pili and fimbriae mediate bacterial adhesion; specialized conjugative pili also participate in transfer of genetic material.","E":"Outer membrane does not provide the best response to this question. The deciding principle is: Pili and fimbriae mediate bacterial adhesion; specialized conjugative pili also participate in transfer of genetic material."},"references":[],"review":"model_reasoned_answer_draft_original_source_key_not_consulted","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","provisional":true,"questionTextHash":"d4e40b3cdfb177dafa2175d26a4bf2ba5863340872c015bd2dc3b9a89d07ff4b"},"q-c304ce4d126c":{"correct":"E","answerText":"TAP.","explanation":"TAP deficiency impairs class I MHC peptide presentation and CD8 T-cell development rather than typically causing a combined B-cell and T-cell immunodeficiency.","optionReasons":{"A":"a thymus. does not provide the best response to this question. The deciding principle is: TAP deficiency impairs class I MHC peptide presentation and CD8 T-cell development rather than typically causing a combined B-cell and T-cell immunodeficiency.","B":"Class II MHC. does not provide the best response to this question. The deciding principle is: TAP deficiency impairs class I MHC peptide presentation and CD8 T-cell development rather than typically causing a combined B-cell and T-cell immunodeficiency.","C":"HIV infection of CD4 T cells. does not provide the best response to this question. The deciding principle is: TAP deficiency impairs class I MHC peptide presentation and CD8 T-cell development rather than typically causing a combined B-cell and T-cell immunodeficiency.","D":"RAG-1 or RAG-2 products. does not provide the best response to this question. The deciding principle is: TAP deficiency impairs class I MHC peptide presentation and CD8 T-cell development rather than typically causing a combined B-cell and T-cell immunodeficiency.","E":"TAP deficiency impairs class I MHC peptide presentation and CD8 T-cell development rather than typically causing a combined B-cell and T-cell immunodeficiency."},"references":[],"review":"model_reasoned_answer_draft_original_source_key_not_consulted","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","provisional":true,"questionTextHash":"2ccd678d199dac603e02547a9280a9b74be9723bcd856dbad39af188b1324809"},"q-88f068e1a3ac":{"correct":"C","answerText":"B cells bind only processed antigen epitopes.","explanation":"B cells can bind intact native antigen through surface immunoglobulin; processing is not a prerequisite for initial B-cell antigen binding.","optionReasons":{"A":"An epitope may be shared by two different antigens. does not provide the best response to this question. The deciding principle is: B cells can bind intact native antigen through surface immunoglobulin; processing is not a prerequisite for initial B-cell antigen binding.","B":"A protein molecule usually contains multiple epitopes. does not provide the best response to this question. The deciding principle is: B cells can bind intact native antigen through surface immunoglobulin; processing is not a prerequisite for initial B-cell antigen binding.","C":"B cells can bind intact native antigen through surface immunoglobulin; processing is not a prerequisite for initial B-cell antigen binding.","D":"Epitopes may be linear or assembled. does not provide the best response to this question. The deciding principle is: B cells can bind intact native antigen through surface immunoglobulin; processing is not a prerequisite for initial B-cell antigen binding.","E":"Some epitopes are more immunogenic than others does not provide the best response to this question. The deciding principle is: B cells can bind intact native antigen through surface immunoglobulin; processing is not a prerequisite for initial B-cell antigen binding."},"references":[],"review":"model_reasoned_answer_draft_original_source_key_not_consulted","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","provisional":true,"questionTextHash":"5be2ff58c043ab2e03ba72c9a04b2b2cd05e3e61eab9f5b9988d8a07725f05aa"},"q-ba2e0c518bf8":{"correct":"E","answerText":"presenting antigen to B cells.","explanation":"Complement supports opsonization, inflammatory recruitment and membrane-attack-complex lysis, but antigen presentation to B cells is not a primary effector function of complement.","optionReasons":{"A":"attracting phagocytes to the site of infection. does not provide the best response to this question. The deciding principle is: Complement supports opsonization, inflammatory recruitment and membrane-attack-complex lysis, but antigen presentation to B cells is not a primary effector function of complement.","B":"facilitating phagocytosis of complement-coated bacteria. does not provide the best response to this question. The deciding principle is: Complement supports opsonization, inflammatory recruitment and membrane-attack-complex lysis, but antigen presentation to B cells is not a primary effector function of complement.","C":"increasing blood vessel permeability to plasma proteins. does not provide the best response to this question. The deciding principle is: Complement supports opsonization, inflammatory recruitment and membrane-attack-complex lysis, but antigen presentation to B cells is not a primary effector function of complement.","D":"lysing bacterial cells. does not provide the best response to this question. The deciding principle is: Complement supports opsonization, inflammatory recruitment and membrane-attack-complex lysis, but antigen presentation to B cells is not a primary effector function of complement.","E":"Complement supports opsonization, inflammatory recruitment and membrane-attack-complex lysis, but antigen presentation to B cells is not a primary effector function of complement."},"references":[],"review":"model_reasoned_answer_draft_original_source_key_not_consulted","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","provisional":true,"questionTextHash":"2f7ef3ba5dd0bf68a65c1da2c38b53678f87f0b1ca9ba9ebe29873f2c6b5efff"},"q-6f370bd2b98c":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Hypotension","explanation":"High systemic endotoxin exposure can provoke severe vasodilation and hypotension characteristic of septic shock, beyond the lower-dose inflammatory response.","optionReasons":{"A":"Fever does not provide the best response to this question. The deciding principle is: High systemic endotoxin exposure can provoke severe vasodilation and hypotension characteristic of septic shock, beyond the lower-dose inflammatory response.","B":"Vasodilation does not provide the best response to this question. The deciding principle is: High systemic endotoxin exposure can provoke severe vasodilation and hypotension characteristic of septic shock, beyond the lower-dose inflammatory response.","C":"High systemic endotoxin exposure can provoke severe vasodilation and hypotension characteristic of septic shock, beyond the lower-dose inflammatory response.","D":"Inflammatory response does not provide the best response to this question. The deciding principle is: High systemic endotoxin exposure can provoke severe vasodilation and hypotension characteristic of septic shock, beyond the lower-dose inflammatory response.","E":"Immune response does not provide the best response to this question. The deciding principle is: High systemic endotoxin exposure can provoke severe vasodilation and hypotension characteristic of septic shock, beyond the lower-dose inflammatory response."},"references":[],"review":"model_reasoned_answer_draft_original_source_key_not_consulted","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","provisional":true,"questionTextHash":"0187ec0fc4e3b2511ef0935785455e0ebbddfcc0f291fa18e7d9e582eda146bd"},"q-55302dbe9561":{"correct":"E","answerText":"both c and d are true.","explanation":"Both immunoglobulin V(D)J rearrangement and accompanying irreversible DNA deletion distinguish rearranged lymphocytes from ordinary somatic cells.","optionReasons":{"A":"each polypeptide chain is encoded by several exons. does not provide the best response to this question. The deciding principle is: Both immunoglobulin V(D)J rearrangement and accompanying irreversible DNA deletion distinguish rearranged lymphocytes from ordinary somatic cells.","B":"Ig genes are composed of introns and exons does not provide the best response to this question. The deciding principle is: Both immunoglobulin V(D)J rearrangement and accompanying irreversible DNA deletion distinguish rearranged lymphocytes from ordinary somatic cells.","C":"somatic recombination occurs before mRNA is transcribed does not provide the best response to this question. The deciding principle is: Both immunoglobulin V(D)J rearrangement and accompanying irreversible DNA deletion distinguish rearranged lymphocytes from ordinary somatic cells.","D":"there 