tion. Distinguishing fact: In a patent ductus arteriosus, higher aortic pressure ordinarily drives blood from the aorta into the pulmonary artery, producing a left-to-right shunt.","D":"right atrium — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: In a patent ductus arteriosus, higher aortic pressure ordinarily drives blood from the aorta into the pulmonary artery, producing a left-to-right shunt.","E":"left atrium — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: In a patent ductus arteriosus, higher aortic pressure ordinarily drives blood from the aorta into the pulmonary artery, producing a left-to-right shunt."},"references":[],"review":"independently_reasoned_batch3_provisional_no_original_key","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","provisional":true,"questionTextHash":"cc34173727f10321a22d6ad4e68d417cb137ba48504c6715d213f16f6bf39436"},"q-e253ee54ec4c":{"correct":"D","answerText":"The body’s inability to prevent water loss","explanation":"Extensive burns permit profound fluid loss and expose underlying tissue to infection, leading to hypovolemia and systemic complications.","optionReasons":{"A":"The body’s inability to thermoregulate. — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: Extensive burns permit profound fluid loss and expose underlying tissue to infection, leading to hypovolemia and systemic complications.","B":"The loss of the ability to produce vitamin D. — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: Extensive burns permit profound fluid loss and expose underlying tissue to infection, leading to hypovolemia and systemic complications.","C":"The buildup of urea and uric acid which would otherwise have been excreted by the skin. — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: Extensive burns permit profound fluid loss and expose underlying tissue to infection, leading to hypovolemia and systemic complications.","D":"Extensive burns permit profound fluid loss and expose underlying tissue to infection, leading to hypovolemia and systemic complications."},"references":[],"review":"independently_reasoned_batch3_provisional_no_original_key","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","provisional":true,"questionTextHash":"b6889d8668b7874968245e1f5cb231a60ea872a562eae7c17465f60bc5902f32"},"q-a23c80ac07f0":{"correct":"C","answerText":"polar head and non-polar tail","explanation":"Phospholipids are amphipathic: a hydrophilic phosphate-containing head and hydrophobic fatty-acid tails form the cell-membrane bilayer.","optionReasons":{"A":"polar head and polar tail — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: Phospholipids are amphipathic: a hydrophilic phosphate-containing head and hydrophobic fatty-acid tails form the cell-membrane bilayer.","B":"non-polar head and a polar tail — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: Phospholipids are amphipathic: a hydrophilic phosphate-containing head and hydrophobic fatty-acid tails form the cell-membrane bilayer.","C":"Phospholipids are amphipathic: a hydrophilic phosphate-containing head and hydrophobic fatty-acid tails form the cell-membrane bilayer.","D":"non-polar head and a non-polar tail — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: Phospholipids are amphipathic: a hydrophilic phosphate-containing head and hydrophobic fatty-acid tails form the cell-membrane bilayer."},"references":[],"review":"independently_reasoned_batch3_provisional_no_original_key","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","provisional":true,"questionTextHash":"0f887076a54218a6a3b8f75cbd3906e8a2b03093963243b3e5181e30c384f0f8"},"q-660b6b394852":{"correct":"A","answerText":"In the dermis","explanation":"The dermis is predominantly collagen-rich connective tissue; epithelial tissue forms the overlying epidermis, glands and vascular endothelium.","optionReasons":{"A":"The dermis is predominantly collagen-rich connective tissue; epithelial tissue forms the overlying epidermis, glands and vascular endothelium.","B":"In exocrine glands — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: The dermis is predominantly collagen-rich connective tissue; epithelial tissue forms the overlying epidermis, glands and vascular endothelium.","C":"In endocrine glands — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: The dermis is predominantly collagen-rich connective tissue; epithelial tissue forms the overlying epidermis, glands and vascular endothelium.","D":"In the endothelium of blood vessels — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: The dermis is predominantly collagen-rich connective tissue; epithelial tissue forms the overlying epidermis, glands and vascular endothelium."},"references":[],"review":"independently_reasoned_batch3_provisional_no_original_key","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","provisional":true,"questionTextHash":"d87a633e6344cff3982d8edc0aff15902250605c0ebd4a5b6ae5005c9d369b7f"},"q-ce291c8b36b5":{"correct":"E","answerText":"Diffuse lymphatic tissue","explanation":"Diffuse lymphatic tissue describes scattered lymphocytes in mucosal and other connective tissues without a discrete capsule or organized follicle.","optionReasons":{"A":"Lymph follicles — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: Diffuse lymphatic tissue describes scattered lymphocytes in mucosal and other connective tissues without a discrete capsule or organized follicle.","B":"White pulp — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: Diffuse lymphatic tissue describes scattered lymphocytes in mucosal and other connective tissues without a discrete capsule or organized follicle.","C":"Peyer's patches — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: Diffuse lymphatic tissue describes scattered lymphocytes in mucosal and other connective tissues without a discrete capsule or organized follicle.","D":"Lymph node — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: Diffuse lymphatic tissue describes scattered lymphocytes in mucosal and other connective tissues without a discrete capsule or organized follicle.","E":"Diffuse lymphatic tissue describes scattered lymphocytes in mucosal and other connective tissues without a discrete capsule or organized follicle."},"references":[],"review":"independently_reasoned_batch3_provisional_no_original_key","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","provisional":true,"questionTextHash":"6a55fb7b8d62e5ef5bed2c8ad79144a3aece6f99bde8c3422508138718b8b2ff"},"q-cbe515765e62":{"correct":"C","answerText":"A buffer is a weak base and its acid salt or a weak acid and its basic salt. The ratio of the two components helps maintain blood pH levels.","explanation":"A classical buffer contains a weak acid with its conjugate base, or a weak base with its conjugate acid; this resists abrupt pH changes.","optionReasons":{"A":"A buffer is any acid and base which together control the concentration of pH in the blood. — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: A classical buffer contains a weak acid with its conjugate base, or a weak base with its conjugate acid; this resists abrupt pH changes.","B":"A buffer is the solution which allows CO2 to be lost from the lungs in order to control the pH of the lungs. — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: A classical buffer contains a weak acid with its conjugate base, or a weak base with its conjugate acid; this resists abrupt pH changes.","C":"A classical buffer contains a weak acid with its conjugate base, or a weak base with its conjugate acid; this resists abrupt pH changes.","D":"A buffer is a mixture of two acids which together help to maintain blood pH — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: A classical buffer contains a weak acid with its conjugate base, or a weak base with its conjugate acid; this resists abrupt pH changes."},"references":[],"review":"independently_reasoned_batch3_provisional_no_original_key","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","provisional":true,"questionTextHash":"d9e125b75ccdeb1107edb3ed3ed239c113bb1e8c75344d7f13a07ad99b4088ba"},"q-3e6ab7f23bff":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Epinephrine","explanation":"Epinephrine is the catecholamine used in cardiac arrest algorithms because it produces alpha-adrenergic vasoconstriction and supports coronary and cerebral perfusion pressure.","optionReasons":{"A":"Methoxamine — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: Epinephrine is the catecholamine used in cardiac arrest algorithms because it produces alpha-adrenergic vasoconstriction and supports coronary and cerebral perfusion pressure.","B":"Phenylephrine — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: Epinephrine is the catecholamine used in cardiac arrest algorithms because it produces alpha-adrenergic vasoconstriction and supports coronary and cerebral perfusion pressure.","C":"Epinephrine is the catecholamine used in cardiac arrest algorithms because it produces alpha-adrenergic vasoconstriction and supports coronary and cerebral perfusion pressure.","D":"Xylometazoline — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: Epinephrine is the catecholamine used in cardiac arrest algorithms because it produces alpha-adrenergic vasoconstriction and supports coronary and cerebral perfusion pressure."},"references":[],"review":"independently_reasoned_batch3_provisional_no_original_key","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","provisional":true,"questionTextHash":"2515f346e2f8e2b7de1930c7093dee1ee11d408f8df17a00b94d82cab162e632"},"q-15f782d097d2":{"correct":"B","answerText":"required for uptake of calcium from the gut","explanation":"Active vitamin D increases gastrointestinal absorption of calcium and phosphate and contributes to mineral homeostasis.","optionReasons":{"A":"required for several stages of haemostasis — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: Active vitamin D increases gastrointestinal absorption of calcium and phosphate and contributes to mineral homeostasis.","B":"Active vitamin D increases gastrointestinal absorption of calcium and phosphate and contributes to mineral homeostasis.","C":"required for erythropoiesis — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: Active vitamin D increases gastrointestinal absorption of calcium and phosphate and contributes to mineral homeostasis.","D":"required for uptake of intrinsic factor — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: Active vitamin D increases gastrointestinal absorption of calcium and phosphate and contributes to mineral homeostasis."},"references":[],"review":"independently_reasoned_batch3_provisional_no_original_key","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","provisional":true,"questionTextHash":"ba41a74cb12209c7fe4eef8e77d4b9ceb566372b6808efb23918e9f8822e6289"},"q-1b023bea3218":{"correct":"D","answerText":"Faeces","explanation":"Trichomonas vaginalis infects the urogenital tract; feces are not an appropriate specimen for its recovery.","optionReasons":{"A":"Urine — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: Trichomonas vaginalis infects the urogenital tract; feces are not an appropriate specimen for its recovery.","B":"Urethral discharge — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: Trichomonas vaginalis infects the urogenital tract; feces are not an appropriate specimen for its recovery.","C":"Prostatic discharge — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: Trichomonas vaginalis infects the urogenital tract; feces are not an appropriate specimen for its recovery.","D":"Trichomonas vaginalis infects the urogenital tract; feces are not an appropriate specimen for its recovery.","E":"All of the above — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: Trichomonas vaginalis infects the urogenital tract; feces are not an appropriate specimen for its recovery."},"references":[],"review":"independently_reasoned_batch3_provisional_no_original_key","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","provisional":true,"questionTextHash":"74b83e00a914da4424e416b85f9494b6f6da02c24cfbbd106bee63bd73352daf"},"q-17cacfb763e1":{"correct":"D","answerText":"The salt of the drug may be more soluble in water than the acid form of the drug.","explanation":"Ionized salt forms of many acidic medicines are more water-soluble than the nonionized parent acid, though this depends on formulation and pH.","optionReasons":{"A":"The salt of the drug does not carry an electrical charge but the acid form does. — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: Ionized salt forms of many acidic medicines are more water-soluble than the nonionized parent acid, though this depends on formulation and pH.","B":"The salt of the drug may be more soluble in lipid than the acid form of the drug. — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: Ionized salt forms of many acidic medicines are more water-soluble than the nonionized parent acid, though this depends on formulation and pH.","C":"The acid form of the drug is ionised whereas the salt is not. — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: Ionized salt forms of many acidic medicines are more water-soluble than the nonionized parent acid, though this depends on formulation and pH.","D":"Ionized salt forms of many acidic medicines are more water-soluble than the nonionized parent acid, though this depends on formulation and pH."},"references":[],"review":"independently_reasoned_batch3_provisional_no_original_key","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","provisional":true,"questionTextHash":"db1c91ef241ead84f37e2df0e7c75e12fb193a26ca7891f5ba4290a7acf42fd8"},"q-4b2aa2bb86c5":{"correct":"B","answerText":"Bicipital groove separates the greater and lesser tubercles.","explanation":"The intertubercular sulcus of the humerus lies between its greater and lesser tubercles and carries the tendon of the long head of biceps.","optionReasons":{"A":"Lesser tubercle has three facets. — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: The intertubercular sulcus of the humerus lies between its greater and lesser tubercles and carries the tendon of the long head of biceps.","B":"The intertubercular sulcus of the humerus lies between its greater and lesser tubercles and carries the tendon of the long head of biceps.","C":"Surgical neck separates the head from the tubercles. — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: The intertubercular sulcus of the humerus lies between its greater and lesser tubercles and carries the tendon of the long head of biceps.","D":"Common extensor origin is the medial epicondyle. — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: The intertubercular sulcus of the humerus lies between its greater and lesser tubercles and carries the tendon of the long head of biceps.","E":"Capitulum articulates with the ulna. — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: The intertubercular sulcus of the humerus lies between its greater and lesser tubercles and carries the tendon of the long head of biceps."},"references":[],"review":"independently_reasoned_batch3_provisional_no_original_key","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","provisional":true,"questionTextHash":"4aeba81c5b8e83958027c2a90547d519cf4cd4b2b870987221bc0fb57c27c84b"},"q-e8332c3a3887":{"correct":"A","answerText":"AZT","explanation":"Zidovudine (AZT) is associated with bone-marrow suppression, including clinically important anemia, which can cause fatigue and pallor during antiretroviral therapy.","optionReasons":{"A":"Zidovudine (AZT) is associated with bone-marrow suppression, including clinically important anemia, which can cause fatigue and pallor during antiretroviral therapy.","B":"3TC — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: Zidovudine (AZT) is associated with bone-marrow suppression, including clinically important anemia, which can cause fatigue and pallor during antiretroviral therapy.","C":"FTC — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: Zidovudine (AZT) is associated with bone-marrow suppression, including clinically important anemia, which can cause fatigue and pallor during antiretroviral therapy.","D":"ABC — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: Zidovudine (AZT) is associated with bone-marrow suppression, including clinically important anemia, which can cause fatigue and pallor during antiretroviral therapy.","E":"D4T — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: Zidovudine (AZT) is associated with bone-marrow suppression, including clinically important anemia, which can cause fatigue and pallor during antiretroviral therapy."},"references":[],"review":"independently_reasoned_batch3_provisional_no_original_key","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","provisional":true,"questionTextHash":"4fd642c5655e1e4c1d6a42b27d6af1525b1b09f2b2374a6dfd6f7d91aef0e30a"},"q-301f2b66b7ff":{"correct":"A","answerText":"Atracurium","explanation":"Atracurium undergoes substantial organ-independent Hofmann elimination and ester hydrolysis, making it useful when renal clearance is impaired.","optionReasons":{"A":"Atracurium undergoes substantial organ-independent Hofmann elimination and ester hydrolysis, making it useful when renal clearance is impaired.","B":"Succinylcholine — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: Atracurium undergoes substantial organ-independent Hofmann elimination and ester hydrolysis, making it useful when renal clearance is impaired.","C":"Pipecuronium — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: Atracurium undergoes substantial organ-independent Hofmann elimination and ester hydrolysis, making it useful when renal clearance is impaired.","D":"Doxacurium — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: Atracurium undergoes substantial organ-independent Hofmann elimination and ester hydrolysis, making it useful when renal clearance is impaired."},"references":[],"review":"independently_reasoned_batch3_provisional_no_original_key","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","provisional":true,"questionTextHash":"2eca567d86118ad321b85222049450c6fed9c8c5bad167ac276f6b457491aa60"},"q-2d89daa0669a":{"correct":"D","answerText":"fluid loss and loss of the barrier against infection","explanation":"Extensive full-thickness burns compromise the water barrier and protection against pathogens, creating immediate risks of hypovolemia and infection.","optionReasons":{"A":"fluid loss and inability to produce vitamin D. — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: Extensive full-thickness burns compromise the water barrier and protection against pathogens, creating immediate risks of hypovolemia and infection.","B":"loss of ability to thermoregulate and infection. — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: Extensive full-thickness burns compromise the water barrier and protection against pathogens, creating immediate risks of hypovolemia and infection.","C":"inability to excrete lactic acid, urea and uric acid, loss of thermoregulation. — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: Extensive full-thickness burns compromise the water barrier and protection against pathogens, creating immediate risks of hypovolemia and infection.","D":"Extensive full-thickness burns compromise the water barrier and protection against pathogens, creating immediate risks of hypovolemia and infection."},"references":[],"review":"independently_reasoned_batch3_provisional_no_original_key","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","provisional":true,"questionTextHash":"397574a840c87678b221eb99a495edd719634f243fc54cc049b1a624902d3173"},"q-e444ea182be1":{"correct":"E","answerText":"All of the above","explanation":"Peripheral nerves, peripheral ganglia, sensory receptors and nerve plexuses all 