rly collagen, reticular and elastic fibers.","C":"The extracellular matrix of connective tissue consists of ground substance and extracellular protein fibers, particularly collagen, reticular and elastic fibers.","D":"Interstitial fluid — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: The extracellular matrix of connective tissue consists of ground substance and extracellular protein fibers, particularly collagen, reticular and elastic fibers."},"references":[],"review":"independently_reasoned_batch3_provisional_no_original_key","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","provisional":true,"questionTextHash":"e3801a1c8bdc56735fba58632d4573b3e6daeb7fc79772cda18f8ddb05732e20"},"q-33f035ed6a61":{"correct":"E","answerText":"Diffuse lymphatic tissue","explanation":"Diffuse mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue contains scattered lymphocytes rather than an organized follicle, lymph node or white-pulp compartment.","optionReasons":{"A":"Lymph follicles — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: Diffuse mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue contains scattered lymphocytes rather than an organized follicle, lymph node or white-pulp compartment.","B":"White pulp — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: Diffuse mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue contains scattered lymphocytes rather than an organized follicle, lymph node or white-pulp compartment.","C":"Peyer's patches — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: Diffuse mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue contains scattered lymphocytes rather than an organized follicle, lymph node or white-pulp compartment.","D":"Lymph node — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: Diffuse mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue contains scattered lymphocytes rather than an organized follicle, lymph node or white-pulp compartment.","E":"Diffuse mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue contains scattered lymphocytes rather than an organized follicle, lymph node or white-pulp compartment."},"references":[],"review":"independently_reasoned_batch3_provisional_no_original_key","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","provisional":true,"questionTextHash":"6a55fb7b8d62e5ef5bed2c8ad79144a3aece6f99bde8c3422508138718b8b2ff"},"q-54e630643e7b":{"correct":"D","answerText":"All of the above","explanation":"Typical local-anesthetic molecules contain a lipophilic aromatic group, an intermediate ester or amide linkage and a hydrophilic amino group.","optionReasons":{"A":"Lipophylic group (frequently an aromatic ring) — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: Typical local-anesthetic molecules contain a lipophilic aromatic group, an intermediate ester or amide linkage and a hydrophilic amino group.","B":"Intermediate chain (commonly including an ester or amide) — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: Typical local-anesthetic molecules contain a lipophilic aromatic group, an intermediate ester or amide linkage and a hydrophilic amino group.","C":"Amino group — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: Typical local-anesthetic molecules contain a lipophilic aromatic group, an intermediate ester or amide linkage and a hydrophilic amino group.","D":"Typical local-anesthetic molecules contain a lipophilic aromatic group, an intermediate ester or amide linkage and a hydrophilic amino group."},"references":[],"review":"independently_reasoned_batch3_provisional_no_original_key","reviewDate":"2026-10-19","provisional":true,"questionTextHash":"4c29dd98282b47909c680babeaa15ad9fc8a7f0874c6cf491e1b0a64db72933e"},"q-5a190a07952a":{"correct":"C","answerText":"Phaechromocytoma","explanation":"The triad of episodic headache, palpitations and diaphoresis strongly suggests catecholamine-secreting pheochromocytoma, although biochemical confirmation is required.","optionReasons":{"A":"Primary aldosteronism 2 — Not the best choice for the stated question. Distinguishing fact: The triad of episodic heada